# GENERATED by the server when it loads this world. Do not edit.
#
# These are the types the engine hands a script: what a Builder offers, what a Player
# offers, and so on. They are declared and left open because nothing here runs — the real
# members live in the server, and this exists so `cm check` and the VS Code extension can
# see what your rules are talking about.
#
# What each member does is written above it, so hovering one in the editor answers the
# question without leaving the file.

##
    @summary: A handle to somebody in the world. It reads the world live, so a handle held
    across a tick still answers about the person it named. One arrives as a handler's first
    argument or from Builder.Find; a script cannot construct one. Check IsHere before using one
    you have held on to.
##
abstract model Player

    ##
        @summary: Sends a line of text to this player, and to nobody else.
    ##
    public abstract function Message(string line);

    ##
        @summary: Their character's name, trimmed. Blank once the body has left - the same answer a
        creature's name gives, because it is the same question.
    ##
    public string Name;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are still in the world. A handle outlives the body it names.
    ##
    public boolean IsHere;

    ##
        @summary: The name of the guild their account belongs to, or empty for none.
    ##
    public string Guild;

    ##
        @summary: The account behind them - the one name here that outlives a session. A handle
        stops meaning anything the moment they log out and a character can be deleted; this is what
        the engine files mail and guild membership under, so it is what to write down when the thing
        you are promising will be settled later. not a character name and not for showing to
        players: it is how they sign in. Print Name in anything anybody reads.
    ##
    public string Account;

    ##
        @summary: Which map they are standing on, or zero when they are nowhere.
    ##
    public integer Map;

    ##
        @summary: How far across that map they are.
    ##
    public integer X;

    ##
        @summary: The square they are standing on, as a value - map, tile and plane together. What
        to hand anything that puts something where somebody is, because a bridge and the water under
        it are the same three numbers and two different places.
    ##
    public Spot Where;

    ##
        @summary: How far down it.
    ##
    public integer Y;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they carry that key at all, so absence is told from zero.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Has(string key);

    ##
        @summary: What they carry under that key, or zero where they carry nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Number(string key);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text, or empty where they carry nothing.
    ##
    public abstract string function Text(string key);

    ##
        @summary: Writes that key, and ships it to everyone entitled to see it.
    ##
    public abstract function SetNumber(string key, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with text.
    ##
    public abstract function SetText(string key, string value);

    ##
        @summary: Puts them on that map, x and y. False for a square that is not a real tile.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function WarpTo(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: Puts that many of an item in their bag.
    ##
    public abstract function Give(integer item, integer many);

    ##
        @summary: Takes that many out of it, worn ones included.
    ##
    public abstract function Take(integer item, integer many);

    ##
        @summary: How many of that item they are carrying, worn ones and every stack counted
        together. The read that goes with Give and Take: a rule charging somebody in a currency of
        its own asks this first, because taking more than they have and taking what they have look
        the same afterwards.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Carrying(integer item);

    ##
        @summary: Puts something they are carrying on, taking off whatever was in its slot. A game
        that hands somebody a sword has no other way to put it in their hand - an opening kit, a
        quest reward, a curse that arms them against their will. None of the refusals a player
        pressing the button meets apply: a rule arming somebody mid-fight meant to. False for a body
        not carrying it, and for a piece naming a slot this world does not declare.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Wear(integer item);

    ##
        @summary: Takes something off. It stays in the bag. The other half of Wear, and what a rule
        about ruined gear needs: a piece worn down to nothing comes off the body it broke on. False
        for a body not wearing it.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function TakeOff(integer item);

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are running or walking right now. What running costs is up to you;
        the engine moves the body, and this is how a rule finds out.
    ##
    public boolean IsRunning;

    ##
        @summary: Marks them as in a fight for that many seconds. What being in a fight means is the
        game's; the engine keeps the clock and the client shows it.
    ##
    public abstract function Engage(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Marks them as out of the fight for that many seconds.
    ##
    public abstract function Down(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Marks them for that many seconds - a target somebody else's rule put a flag on.
    ##
    public abstract function Mark(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Marks them as the one who started it, for that many seconds. What that costs them
        is the game's to decide.
    ##
    public abstract function Flag(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Holds them off acting again for that many seconds.
    ##
    public abstract function Wait(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Floats a line off them, to everybody who can see it happen - a number, a word, a
        name. The color is red, green and blue, each 0 to 255. The one place a script asks the
        client to draw: everything else it does sets state and lets the client decide what that
        looks like, and a number that happened once is not state.
    ##
    public abstract function Float(string line, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are in a fight right now. What being in one means is yours - holding
        regenthrough it, refusing a warp out of it - and this is the clock you set, read back.
    ##
    public boolean IsEngaged;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are out of action right now.
    ##
    public boolean IsDowned;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they carry a mark right now.
    ##
    public boolean IsMarked;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are flagged as having started it.
    ##
    public boolean IsAggressor;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are still held off acting. Measured forward from when the cooldown
        started, matching the direction the bar drawing it fills.
    ##
    public boolean IsWaiting;

    ##
        @summary: Sweeps a crescent over them, in the direction they are facing. Pass true to fling
        sparks with it, so the swing reads as connecting instead of passing through air. What the
        crescent depicts is up to you: a sword, a claw, a thrown net.
    ##
    public abstract function Sweep(boolean connected);

    ##
        @summary: Throws something at a creature: 'bolt', 'glitter' or 'parcel', and a color. A
        number floated at the same target waits until it lands, so the hit and the damage read as
        one event - which is most of why this is worth using over a bare burst.
    ##
    public abstract function ThrowAtNpc(Npc at, string look, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Throws something at another player: 'bolt', 'glitter' or 'parcel', and a color. A
        number floated at the same target waits until it lands, so the hit and the damage read as
        one event - which is most of why this is worth using over a bare burst.
    ##
    public abstract function ThrowAtPlayer(Player at, string look, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Bursts droplets from them - power is 0 to 100. Deliberately color-blind: blood,
        sparks off an anvil, water and dust are one burst with a different color. Nothing here
        lasts; something still there a minute later is World.Stain.
    ##
    public abstract function Burst(integer red, integer green, integer blue, integer power);

    ##
        @summary: Takes them out of the world, with a cause OnMayDie can read. False when something
        refused the death, which a death policy is allowed to do.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Kill(string cause);

    ##
        @summary: Says where this body comes back. Only from inside OnDied, and only about the body
        that died - it is read the moment that handler returns, and anywhere else it does nothing.
        Say nothing and they come back where the world puts them.
    ##
    public abstract function RespawnAt(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: The body behind a name, or nothing if no one is using it. OnAction hands you a
        name; this turns it into a player you can read and write.
    ##
    public abstract Player? function Find(string name);
end model

##
    @summary: Declares what the game is. Handed to Configure, which runs once before the world
    exists, so nothing here can refer to a player. Calls that hand something back let you
    configure that thing on the lines below.
##
abstract model Builder

    ##
        @summary: Declares a key this game counts, and who may see it: none, owner, or viewport.
    ##
    public abstract function Attribute(string key, string seenBy);

    ##
        @summary: A message a client may send this game, taking its fields from one of this world's
        own models. It arrives at OnMessage with those fields as values. A stock client cannot
        compose one, so this is for a client, a tool, or a bot that knows it.
    ##
    public abstract function Message(string modelName);

    ##
        @summary: How many ticks between calls to OnTick and OnPlayerTick. A tick is 100ms, so the
        default of 1 calls them ten times a second, 10 calls them once a second, and 600 calls them
        once a minute. This sets the rate for the whole module.
    ##
    public abstract function TickEvery(integer ticks);

    ##
        @summary: A place on a body something can be worn. A caption left blank becomes the key, as
        words.
    ##
    public abstract function EquipSlot(string key, string caption);

    ##
        @summary: A heading on the sidebar, separating the rows under it.
    ##
    public abstract function Heading(string caption);

    ##
        @summary: A sidebar row: a key read live off the player, a caption, and a color as red,
        green, and blue. All three zero leaves the color to the client.
    ##
    public abstract function Field(string key, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: The same, drawn as a small tag with no caption.
    ##
    public abstract function Badge(string key, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: A sidebar bar, filled by one key against another.
    ##
    public abstract function Meter(string key, string outOf, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: A bar over every body's head, in a color given as red, green, and blue, each 0 to
        255.
    ##
    public abstract function Bar(string key, string outOf, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: A verb this game offers, under a heading of its own. Picking it calls OnAction.
        Offered in a square's menu until the verb says otherwise, and handed back so where it is
        offered, what key reaches it, and what it needs are each their own line.
    ##
    public abstract Verb function Action(string id, string caption, string heading);

    ##
        @summary: Something to ask before a character exists - a class, a bloodline, a starting
        town. The one question a game cannot ask any other way: everything else it wants to know it
        asks of a body already in the world, and what a character is has to be settled before there
        is one. The player picks from the records you name, listed by their own names, and the
        number they picked is written onto them under your key before OnPlayerJoined runs, so you
        read it the ordinary way and need no handler. A blank record is not offered, because a list
        of unnamed slots is a screen nobody can use.
    ##
    public abstract function AskAtCreation(string key, string caption, string records);

    ##
        @summary: A screen of this game's own: an id, a title, and how wide and tall it is. Handed
        back, so its rows and its buttons are written underneath it.
    ##
    public abstract Panel function Panel(string id, string title, integer width, integer height);
end model

##
    @summary: A kind of record this game authors, handed to a model's Describe. The model's
    fields become the editor form; this adds captions, bounds and the folder the files live in.
    Describe must be shared: it configures the record type, not one record.
##
abstract model Records

    ##
        @summary: What these records are called in the editor — the plural, then the singular — and
        how many there may be. A caption left blank keeps the model's own name.
    ##
    public abstract function Are(string plural, string singular, integer limit);

    ##
        @summary: The glyph beside it. One of: grid, quads, pin, flag, bag, gem, coin, sword, flask,
        cog, person, paw, leaf, heart, book, scroll, list, bubble, key, shield, star, spark, flame,
        clock, note, dice, shop. A name that is not one of those is refused, because a glyph nobody
        drew is a section that looks like every other section.
    ##
    public abstract function Icon(string glyph);

    ##
        @summary: Adds this model's fields to a family that already exists - the engine's 'Items' or
        'NPCs', or another module's - instead of declaring one of its own. This is how a game hangs
        its own facts on a record the engine owns: which classes may wield a sword, which spell is
        written on a scroll. The engine's own item properties are a closed set, because Core cannot
        act on one it has never heard of; yours are open, and they belong on the sword itself rather
        than in a second table keyed by item number. The other family decides what these records are
        called, where they live and how many there may be, so Are and Stored do nothing here.
    ##
    public abstract function Extend(string records);

    ##
        @summary: Where these records live: the folder under the world, and what each file is called
        before its number. Only needed for records already on disk. A new game leaves it out, and
        the model's name decides.
    ##
    public abstract function Stored(string folder, string prefix);

    ##
        @summary: What one field is called on the form. Only needed where the field's own name is
        not the words an author should read.
    ##
    public abstract function Caption(string field, string caption);

    ##
        @summary: The bounds of a whole-number field. Equal bounds mean unbounded.
    ##
    public abstract function Range(string field, integer least, integer greatest);

    ##
        @summary: How long a text field may be. Zero means no limit.
    ##
    public abstract function Length(string field, integer characters);

    ##
        @summary: Makes a whole-number field a picker over another kind of record, listing them by
        name. Use it to point at the engine's own records - 'Items', 'NPCs', 'Maps', 'Shops',
        'Conversations' - which have no model to type a field as. A field typed as one of your own
        models already does this and needs nothing here. The number is still what is stored: this
        changes what the form draws and nothing about what a rule reads back.
    ##
    public abstract function Points(string field, string records);
end model

##
    @summary: An action the player can take, handed back by Builder.Action. Set where it is
    offered, what key reaches it and what it requires on separate lines, in any order. Picking
    it calls OnAction with the verb's id.
##
abstract model Verb

    ##
        @summary: Offer it in the menu of a square. The default, and where most verbs belong.
    ##
    public abstract function OnTile();

    ##
        @summary: Offer it in the menu of another player, who arrives as 'on' in OnAction.
    ##
    public abstract function OnPlayer();

    ##
        @summary: Offer it in the menu of a creature. Declaring one is what gives a plain creature a
        menu at all.
    ##
    public abstract function OnNpc();

    ##
        @summary: Offer it as a button on the HUD, for a verb about the player rather than about
        something they are pointing at.
    ##
    public abstract function OnHud();

    ##
        @summary: A key that reaches it without the menu: B, E, J, K, N, P, Q, R, T, U, Y, or Z. The
        key acts on the square the player faces.
    ##
    public abstract function Key(string key);

    ##
        @summary: The glyph beside it. One of: grid, quads, pin, flag, bag, gem, coin, sword, flask,
        cog, person, paw, leaf, heart, book, scroll, list, bubble, key, shield, star, spark, flame,
        clock, note, dice, shop. A name that is not one of those is refused, because a glyph nobody
        drew is a section that looks like every other section.
    ##
    public abstract function Icon(string glyph);

    ##
        @summary: Picking it also does what the engine's own reach key would have done - a shop, a
        conversation, or the body's own line. A game that binds E takes that key outright, so this
        is how it hands interaction back. Only meaningful on a creature.
    ##
    public abstract function Interacts();

    ##
        @summary: The panel it opens, by the id given to game.Panel. A panel that was never declared
        is refused by name, so you get an error instead of a button that does nothing.
    ##
    public abstract function Opens(string panel);

    ##
        @summary: Offered only to a body carrying at least that much under that key. Below it the
        entry is grayed out instead of hidden, so a player can still see the verb exists.
    ##
    public abstract function NeedsAtLeast(string key, integer least);

    ##
        @summary: Offered only to a body that carries that key at all.
    ##
    public abstract function NeedsCarrying(string key);

    ##
        @summary: Offered only to a body that does not carry that key.
    ##
    public abstract function NeedsNothing(string key);
end model

##
    @summary: A screen of this game's own, handed back by Builder.Panel. Add its rows, buttons
    and forms on the lines below. A stock client draws it with no build of its own: rows read
    live off the player, and Asks turns a model into a form the player can fill in and send.
##
abstract model Panel

    ##
        @summary: A key that opens it: B, E, J, K, N, P, Q, R, T, U, Y, or Z.
    ##
    public abstract function Key(string key);

    ##
        @summary: A button along its bottom: a caption, and the id of a verb it calls.
    ##
    public abstract function Button(string caption, string verb);

    ##
        @summary: The glyph beside it. One of: grid, quads, pin, flag, bag, gem, coin, sword, flask,
        cog, person, paw, leaf, heart, book, scroll, list, bubble, key, shield, star, spark, flame,
        clock, note, dice, shop. A name that is not one of those is refused, because a glyph nobody
        drew is a section that looks like every other section.
    ##
    public abstract function Icon(string glyph);

    ##
        @summary: Asks the player to fill one of this world's own models in, and send it. Every
        field of the model becomes a control — a number a spinner, a truth a checkbox, an
        enumeration a drop-down over its members — and the caption names the button under them. What
        they send arrives at OnMessage under the model's name. A panel asks for one message.
    ##
    public abstract function Asks(string modelName, string caption);

    ##
        @summary: A heading on this panel, separating the rows under it.
    ##
    public abstract function Heading(string caption);

    ##
        @summary: A row on this panel: a key read live off the player, a caption, and a color as
        red, green, and blue. All three zero leaves the color to the client.
    ##
    public abstract function Field(string key, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: The same, drawn as a small tag with no caption.
    ##
    public abstract function Badge(string key, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: A bar on this panel, filled by one key against another.
    ##
    public abstract function Meter(string key, string outOf, string caption, integer red, integer green, integer blue);
end model

##
    @summary: A handle to a creature, the counterpart of Player. Get one from World.NpcAt; a
    script cannot construct one. A handle identifies a creature by its spawn post, not its
    current tile, so it stays valid when the creature walks onto another map. Check IsHere
    before using one you have held on to.
##
abstract model Npc

    ##
        @summary: What it is called - the name on its record, trimmed. Blank once the body has left.
    ##
    public string Name;

    ##
        @summary: Whether the body is still in the world. A handle outlives what it names.
    ##
    public boolean IsHere;

    ##
        @summary: Which map it is standing on, or zero when it is nowhere.
    ##
    public integer Map;

    ##
        @summary: How far across that map it is.
    ##
    public integer X;

    ##
        @summary: The square it is standing on, as a value - map, tile and plane together. What to
        hand anything that puts something where a body is, because a bridge and the water under it
        are the same three numbers and two different places.
    ##
    public Spot Where;

    ##
        @summary: How far down it.
    ##
    public integer Y;

    ##
        @summary: Whether it carries that key at all, so absence is told from zero.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Has(string key);

    ##
        @summary: What it carries under that key, or zero where it carries nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Number(string key);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text, or empty where it carries nothing.
    ##
    public abstract string function Text(string key);

    ##
        @summary: Writes that key, and ships it to everyone entitled to see it.
    ##
    public abstract function SetNumber(string key, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with text.
    ##
    public abstract function SetText(string key, string value);

    ##
        @summary: Floats a line off them, to everybody who can see it happen - a number, a word, a
        name. The color is red, green and blue, each 0 to 255. The one place a script asks the
        client to draw: everything else it does sets state and lets the client decide what that
        looks like, and a number that happened once is not state.
    ##
    public abstract function Float(string line, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are in a fight right now. What being in one means is yours - holding
        regenthrough it, refusing a warp out of it - and this is the clock you set, read back.
    ##
    public boolean IsEngaged;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they carry a mark right now.
    ##
    public boolean IsMarked;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are flagged as having started it.
    ##
    public boolean IsAggressor;

    ##
        @summary: Whether they are still held off acting. Measured forward from when the cooldown
        started, matching the direction the bar drawing it fills.
    ##
    public boolean IsWaiting;

    ##
        @summary: Marks it as in a fight for that many seconds, which brings up its overhead bars.
        What being in a fight means is the game's; the engine keeps the clock.
    ##
    public abstract function Engage(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Marks it for that many seconds - a flag a game puts on a body and reads back
        later, which is how a kill gets claimed by whoever earned it.
    ##
    public abstract function Mark(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Marks it as the one that started it, for that many seconds.
    ##
    public abstract function Flag(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Holds it off acting again for that many seconds.
    ##
    public abstract function Wait(integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: Sweeps a crescent over them, in the direction they are facing. Pass true to fling
        sparks with it, so the swing reads as connecting instead of passing through air. What the
        crescent depicts is up to you: a sword, a claw, a thrown net.
    ##
    public abstract function Sweep(boolean connected);

    ##
        @summary: Throws something at a player: 'bolt', 'glitter' or 'parcel', and a color. A number
        floated at the same target waits until it lands, so the hit and the damage read as one event
        - which is most of why this is worth using over a bare burst.
    ##
    public abstract function ThrowAtPlayer(Player at, string look, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Throws something at another creature: 'bolt', 'glitter' or 'parcel', and a color.
        A number floated at the same target waits until it lands, so the hit and the damage read as
        one event - which is most of why this is worth using over a bare burst.
    ##
    public abstract function ThrowAtNpc(Npc at, string look, integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Bursts droplets from them - power is 0 to 100. Deliberately color-blind: blood,
        sparks off an anvil, water and dust are one burst with a different color. Nothing here
        lasts; something still there a minute later is World.Stain.
    ##
    public abstract function Burst(integer red, integer green, integer blue, integer power);

    ##
        @summary: Takes it out of the world, with a cause the death policy can read. False for a
        body that was not there, or that something refused to let die.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Kill(string cause);

    ##
        @summary: Which creature it is a copy of - the number of its record in this world's
        creatures. What a rule about a species keys on: a bounty per creature, a drop table, which
        bodies a quest counts. Name answers with what a player reads, and two records may share a
        name, so a rule written against one silently follows an editor rename. Zero for a body that
        has left the world.
    ##
    public integer Kind;

    ##
        @summary: Sends it after a player, whether or not it would have noticed them on its own.
        This is how you write a creature that only fights back: author it to amble, then chase
        whoever hits it. It runs the approach rather than walking in, because it was sent rather
        than tempted. It overrides the noticing and not the legs, so one authored to hold its tile
        still holds it and one authored to open the gap runs away instead. False when either body
        has left the world.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Chase(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: The same, at another creature - a guard sent at whatever wandered in, a beast set
        on one it would have ignored. False for a creature sent after itself.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function ChaseNpc(Npc other);

    ##
        @summary: Lets go of whatever it was chasing, leaving it to its record's own behavior again.
        Harmless on a body that was chasing nothing.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Forget();

    ##
        @summary: How it moves on its own: 'stationary', 'wander', 'pursue', 'flee', 'scavenge', or
        'shadow' - which closes to a distance and keeps it. Six ways of walking and deliberately
        nothing about why - a reason is a property of the game, and 'hostile' means nothing in a
        world with no fighting in it. Empty once the body has left.
    ##
    public string Behavior;

    ##
        @summary: Which pack it keeps to, or zero for one in none. Two creatures sharing a group
        never notice each other, on top of never noticing their own kind, so a pack does not fight
        itself.
    ##
    public integer Group;

    ##
        @summary: How far it notices anything, in tiles, as its record was authored. Zero for a body
        that notices nobody.
    ##
    public integer Range;

    ##
        @summary: How many tiles back it holds, for a body that keeps its distance: the authored
        number, or what the engine derives from its reach when the record names none. Zero for every
        other behavior, none of which keeps a distance. Use this to aim rather than a number of your
        own, because the body stops where the engine says it stops.
    ##
    public integer Standoff;

    ##
        @summary: Whether it is after somebody right now - one it noticed, or one you sent it after.
        The other half of Chase and Forget, which write and never read: without this a rule cannot
        tell a creature already in a fight from one standing idle.
    ##
    public boolean IsChasing;

    ##
        @summary: The person it is after, or nothing - which is also the answer when what it is
        after is another creature. The other half of IsChasing: whether a body is after somebody was
        askable and who was not, and a bolt has to be aimed at something.
    ##
    public Player? Target;

    ##
        @summary: And the creature it is after, or nothing. Split in two for the same reason there
        are two contact handlers: a rule handed the wrong kind of body runs and returns nonsense,
        where one that is never called at least returns nothing.
    ##
    public Npc? TargetNpc;
end model

##
    @summary: The live world. Reached by name from any handler; unavailable in Configure, which
    runs before the world exists. Where a handle tells you about a body, World goes the other
    way: give it a square and it tells you who is standing there.
##
shared abstract model World

    ##
        @summary: The creature standing on that square, or nothing. A verb declared OnNpc arrives at
        OnAction with the square it was used on, and this is what turns that into the body.
    ##
    public abstract Npc? function NpcAt(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: Every creature standing within that many tiles of the square, nearest first -
        which is what a rule about the bodies around something starts from: guards answering a call,
        a herd that scatters when one of them is startled. On that map only, so a body one tile over
        a border is close and is not in the answer; ask for each map to reach those.
    ##
    public abstract Npc[] function NpcsNear(integer map, integer x, integer y, integer tiles);

    ##
        @summary: Every creature standing on that map. Use it for a sweep NpcsNear cannot answer:
        telling every creature that night fell, counting what is still alive, clearing something a
        spell left behind. Visitors on the map are included and natives away chasing elsewhere are
        not, so walking every map hands you each creature once.
    ##
    public abstract Npc[] function NpcsOn(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Every player standing within that many tiles of the square, nearest first - the
        mirror of NpcsNear, and what a rule about the people around something starts from: who
        shared a kill, who heard a shout, who was standing too close. On that map only, so somebody
        one tile over a border is close and is not in the answer.
    ##
    public abstract Player[] function PlayersNear(integer map, integer x, integer y, integer tiles);

    ##
        @summary: The player standing on that square, or nothing. Answered before a creature when
        both somehow occupy one tile.
    ##
    public abstract Player? function PlayerAt(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: Says a line to everybody in the world. For the handful of things that are
        genuinely everyone's business - a season turning, somebody finishing what only one person
        can finish. A game that announces ordinary events this way has an unreadable chat log.
    ##
    public abstract function Tell(string line);

    ##
        @summary: Says a line to everybody who can see that map - the nearest thing a seamless world
        has to a room. That is wider than the people standing on it: somebody on the next map along
        is looking at this one too.
    ##
    public abstract function TellOn(integer map, string line);

    ##
        @summary: Says a line to everybody within earshot of a square. A tighter audience than
        TellOn: who can hear speech, not who can see the region.
    ##
    public abstract function TellNear(integer map, integer x, integer y, string line);

    ##
        @summary: Marks the ground. The stain dries on its own and is drawn to everyone who can see
        the tile; amount is 0 to 100. Unlike a burst it persists, so it is still there when somebody
        walks back. The color is the world's, set once for the whole world.
    ##
    public abstract function Stain(integer map, integer x, integer y, integer size, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: Says a line to a set of players, wherever they are. Anybody in the set who has
        since left the world is skipped, not refused, because a set gathered a moment ago may
        already be out of date.
    ##
    public abstract function TellThese(Player[] them, string line);

    ##
        @summary: Everybody in the world who shares their guild, including them. Empty for somebody
        in no guild - which is not the same as a guild with nobody online, and World.Guild is what
        tells those apart.
    ##
    public abstract Player[] function Guildmates(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: Everybody in their party, including them. Empty for somebody in no party.
    ##
    public abstract Player[] function Party(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: What kind of ground is there: 'walkable', 'blocked', 'warp', 'item', 'npcavoid',
        'door', 'plate', or 'ramp'. Empty for a square that is not on a real map. The ground layer -
        a bridge deck is a different answer at the same coordinates.
    ##
    public abstract string function TileAt(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: Whether a straight line between two squares crosses anything that stops sight.
        This is the same trace the client colors its target arrow with, so a rule gating on it
        agrees exactly with what the player was shown. A wall stops sight only if it was authored
        to: a railing blocks walking and not seeing.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function CanSee(integer fromMap, integer fromX, integer fromY, integer toMap, integer toX, integer toY);

    ##
        @summary: How far apart two squares are, in tiles, counting across map borders. The world
        scrolls contiguously, so a body one tile over a border is one tile away; subtracting
        coordinates would call it another map and unreachable. Use this for any range rule. -1 when
        the two are too far apart to compare.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Distance(integer fromMap, integer fromX, integer fromY, integer toMap, integer toX, integer toY);

    ##
        @summary: What time of day it is: 'day', 'dusk', 'night' or 'dawn'. The engine runs the
        cycle and the client paints it. Read it for anything that changes after dark - creatures
        that hunt at night, a shop that shuts, a spell that needs a moon. One answer for the whole
        world, unlike the weather, which is per map.
    ##
    public abstract string function TimeOfDay();

    ##
        @summary: What the sky is doing over that map: 'clear', 'rain', 'snow', 'heatwave', or
        'heavywind'. Empty for a map that is not there.
    ##
    public abstract string function Weather(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Which guild they belong to, as its number, or zero for none. Rules key on the
        number because a guild is a record, not a body: it has no place, nothing walks it, and it
        outlives every member. Player.Guild gives the name, which is for showing people.
    ##
    public abstract integer function GuildOf(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: The guild with that name, or zero. Case-insensitive, the way the engine's own
        founding check compares - so a rule acting on a name a player typed asks the same question
        the engine did when it refused a second guild by that name.
    ##
    public abstract integer function GuildNamed(string name);

    ##
        @summary: What that guild is called, or empty for a number naming none.
    ##
    public abstract string function GuildName(integer guild);

    ##
        @summary: What their account may do to the world: 'player', 'monitor', 'mapper', 'developer'
        or 'creator'. Whoever runs a world usually wants its staff outside its rules - no fighting,
        no loot, no place on a ladder - and which rules that means is yours to decide. Empty for a
        body that is not here.
    ##
    public abstract string function Access(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: What rank they hold: 'leader', 'officer', 'member', or empty for somebody in no
        guild. The engine keeps the rank and moves it; what a rank may do is yours.
    ##
    public abstract string function GuildRank(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: One of the values your game hangs on a guild - a war, a level, a season score.
        Zero for a key it does not carry, and for a number naming no guild.
    ##
    public abstract integer function GuildNumber(integer guild, string key);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text.
    ##
    public abstract string function GuildText(integer guild, string key);

    ##
        @summary: Writes one of them, and gets the guild onto disk. Saved on every write, because a
        guild is not a body: nothing logs it out, so there is no later moment where its values would
        be written anyway.
    ##
    public abstract function SetGuildNumber(integer guild, string key, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with text.
    ##
    public abstract function SetGuildText(integer guild, string key, string value);

    ##
        @summary: Everybody in the world who belongs to that guild. Empty for one with nobody
        online, which is not the same as a guild that is not there.
    ##
    public abstract Player[] function GuildMembers(integer guild);

    ##
        @summary: Every guild there is, by number. what anything ranked starts from: every other
        guild call takes a number you already had, off a body or off a name, and a standing, a
        league table or a sweep over all of them has none. Counting upward and hoping does not work
        either - a guild that disbanded leaves a hole in the numbering.
    ##
    public abstract integer[] function Guilds();

    ##
        @summary: Whoever is signed in to that account right now, or nothing. Use it to reach the
        person again after writing an account down; nothing means they are offline, so post to them
        instead of telling them.
    ##
    public abstract Player? function WhoIs(string account);

    ##
        @summary: Every account in a guild, signed in or not. World.GuildMembers answers only with
        the members currently online; this is the whole roster, which outlives their sessions. Use
        it for anything about the guild itself: a dividend, a census, a rule about who has stopped
        turning up.
    ##
    public abstract string[] function AccountsIn(integer guild);

    ##
        @summary: Whether that account is an active member of the guild or just a name on its
        roster: signed in for long enough, recently enough, by the engine's own measure. Ask it
        before counting somebody - who votes, who makes a quorum, how big a guild really is.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function IsActiveIn(integer guild, string account);

    ##
        @summary: Sends an account a letter, whether or not anybody is signed in to it. What
        World.Mail cannot do: reach somebody who is not here. A rule that wrote an account down when
        it had the person settles up afterwards, and they find it waiting.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function MailTo(string account, string subject, string body);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with something attached. A sale settled, a refund, a prize drawn while
        they were away.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function MailItemTo(string account, integer item, integer many, string subject, string body);

    ##
        @summary: Sends them a letter. the one thing A rule can say that outlives the moment: a line
        of chat is gone when they log out, and a letter waits - through a logout, a restart, and a
        server that was down for a week.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Mail(Player who, string subject, string body);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with an item attached, which waits in the message until it is collected.
        Use it for a refund, a prize, a delivery, or a payout too big for a bag. Player.Give is the
        alternative and needs room in the bag right now.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function MailItem(Player who, integer item, integer many, string subject, string body);

    ##
        @summary: Sends every member of a guild that item, and reaches the ones who are not here.
        The only way to pay somebody offline: everything else reaches a body in the world, and what
        a group earned is owed to its members whether or not they happened to be logged in. It
        arrives as mail, so it waits for them. 'onlyActive' narrows it to members who have really
        been playing, by the engine's own measure of a live roster - a payout split among a hundred
        names nobody has used is a payout nobody feels. Yields how many it reached.
    ##
    public abstract integer function MailMembers(integer guild, integer item, integer many, string subject, string body, boolean onlyActive);

    ##
        @summary: What is in that guild's vault.
    ##
    public abstract integer function GuildGold(integer guild);

    ##
        @summary: Puts gold into a vault. The counterpart to SpendGuildGold: a game holding gold
        aside - an escrow, a stake, a bond - has to be able to give it back.
    ##
    public abstract function GiveGuildGold(integer guild, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: What they are wearing, as item numbers, in slot order. Empty for somebody wearing
        nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer[] function WornBy(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: Which bag slots they have something in, in slot order. Carrying asks about an
        item; this asks about slots, which is what a rule about the bag itself needs - two copies of
        one sword are two slots with their own wear.
    ##
    public abstract integer[] function BagOf(Player who);

    ##
        @summary: What is in that bag slot, by item number. Zero for a slot holding nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function ItemInSlot(Player who, integer slot);

    ##
        @summary: How many that bag slot holds: the stack size for something that stacks, and one
        for anything else. Zero for a slot holding nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function CountInSlot(Player who, integer slot);

    ##
        @summary: Whether that bag slot holds the copy they are wearing. A rule about what a death
        scatters asks this, because worn gear and carried gear are dropped by different rules in
        most games.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function SlotIsWorn(Player who, integer slot);

    ##
        @summary: Puts what is in that bag slot on the ground where they are standing. 'many' takes
        part of a stack; zero takes the whole slot. It lands as a player drop, so anyone may pick it
        up and the world's own limit on litter applies. False for an empty slot.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function DropSlot(Player who, integer slot, integer many);

    ##
        @summary: What they are wearing in one slot, by item number. Zero for an empty slot, and for
        a slot this world does not declare. Ask it instead of walking the whole list when you care
        about one place on the body - whether a shield is up, whether a hand is free.
    ##
    public abstract integer function WornIn(Player who, string slot);

    ##
        @summary: How much wear is left in the copy they are wearing. Zero when they are not wearing
        one - two copies in a bag are two different amounts of wear, and this means the one that was
        on them.
    ##
    public abstract integer function DurabilityLeft(Player who, integer item);

    ##
        @summary: How much that item holds when new. Zero for one with no durability at all, which
        is an ordinary thing for an item to be.
    ##
    public abstract integer function DurabilityFull(Player who, integer item);

    ##
        @summary: Wears out that many points of the copy they are wearing, never past nothing.
        Yields how many were actually taken, which is fewer than asked for when it was nearly worn
        out. An item worn to nothing is not destroyed: it stays in the bag, unusable, until it is
        repaired.
    ##
    public abstract integer function WearOut(Player who, integer item, integer points);

    ##
        @summary: What repairing that many points of that item costs, by the engine's own repair
        rate - the same rate a repair shop charges, so a game pricing wear agrees with the shop.
    ##
    public abstract integer function RepairCost(integer item, integer points);

    ##
        @summary: Puts an item on that square out of nowhere, free to whoever reaches it first. Not
        out of anybody's bag - a chest that opens, a reward left where a quest ended, a hoard a rule
        rolled for itself. Player.Drop is the other one, and it moves something that already exists.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function DropAt(Spot where, integer item, integer many);

    ##
        @summary: The same, held for one player for that many seconds: nobody else may pick it up
        until the time runs out, and the client shows them whose it is. What stops the person who
        did the work watching somebody else walk off with it.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function DropClaimed(Spot where, integer item, integer many, Player who, integer seconds);

    ##
        @summary: What one point of durability costs in gold at that tier, priced off a reference
        piece rather than off any particular item. Fractional on purpose: near the bottom of the
        ladder a point is worth less than a coin. Price other kinds of upkeep - a reagent, a charge,
        a ration - against this, and they will track the repair shop instead of drifting away from
        it.
    ##
    public abstract real function RepairRate(integer tier);

    ##
        @summary: Which map group that map belongs to, or zero. A group is the engine's idea of a
        region: several maps sharing a name and some settings. A game that owns regions asks this to
        turn where somebody is standing into which region it is. Read what your game hangs on one
        with World.Record("MapGroups", ...).
    ##
    public abstract integer function RegionOf(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Where this map puts somebody who leaves it other than by walking, as a map number,
        or zero for one that names none. The map author's say over where leaving this place lands
        you; the engine consults it for nothing on its own, so a game reads it and outranks it as it
        sees fit. Its region answers for a map that says nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function ExitMap(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: How far across that exit is.
    ##
    public abstract integer function ExitX(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: And how far down it.
    ##
    public abstract integer function ExitY(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: One of your own fields on a map, as a whole number - a field you added with
        Records.Extend("Maps"). Answers with the map's own value, falling back to its region's where
        the map leaves it unset, the same way every inherited map property works. Zero when neither
        carries it.
    ##
    public abstract integer function MapNumber(integer map, string field);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text.
    ##
    public abstract string function MapText(integer map, string field);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as a yes or no. An unticked box and an absent one are the same answer.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function MapTruth(integer map, string field);

    ##
        @summary: Writes one of your own fields on a record, and saves it. Where a game keeps what
        belongs to no body and no guild: the last day it settled accounts, a season number, who
        holds a territory. Your own fields only - the engine's properties are written through their
        own paths, which normalize what they are given.
    ##
    public abstract function SetRecordNumber(string records, integer number, string field, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: The same, with text.
    ##
    public abstract function SetRecordText(string records, integer number, string field, string value);

    ##
        @summary: The time now, in seconds since 1970, UTC. What anything dated needs: a cooldown
        that has to survive a restart, a window that stays open for an hour, a daily reset. Counting
        ticks answers a different question, since ticks stop when the server does.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Now();

    ##
        @summary: That square on the ground, as a value - so a rule can carry it around, keep a set
        of them, and hand it to anything that puts something somewhere.
    ##
    public abstract Spot function Spot(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: The same square on the raised surface - a bridge, a ledge, a gantry - instead of
        the ground. One tile, two places.
    ##
    public abstract Spot function SpotRaised(integer map, integer x, integer y);

    ##
        @summary: That many squares spread across a region, every one reachable on foot from every
        other. Measured by walking, across the region's seams - not in a straight line, which is a
        lie wherever a wall or water stands between two tiles that are near on paper, and not by map
        number, which piles everything into whichever corner was drawn first. 'onlyWhere' names one
        of your own truth fields on Maps and a square goes only on a map carrying it; blank puts one
        anywhere in the region. The walk crosses the whole region either way, so a town in the
        middle of one is walked through. Fewer than asked for means there was nowhere else to put
        one.
    ##
    public abstract Spot[] function SpreadOver(integer region, integer count, string onlyWhere);

    ##
        @summary: Takes every creature off that map and keeps it that way. For ground that has to
        stop being ordinary for a while: a war fought over it, a ritual nobody should interrupt, an
        arena cleared for a duel. Nothing comes back until World.Wake - which is what separates this
        from clearing a map and watching it refill a minute later.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Empty(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Lets the map hold creatures again and spawns its own back immediately, instead of
        leaving it bare until each slot's respawn clock comes round.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Refill(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Whether that map is being kept empty of creatures.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function IsEmptied(integer map);

    ##
        @summary: Puts a mark on that square and hands it back, so you can set its ring, label,
        meter and audience on the lines below. The equivalent of an overhead bar, for a place.
        Marking again under a name already in use replaces what is there, which is how a mark moves
        and how its meter advances - one call, not a remove and a place.
    ##
    public abstract Marker function Marker(string id, Spot where);

    ##
        @summary: Takes one away by name. False when nothing was under it, which is an ordinary
        answer for a rule clearing up after something that ended on its own.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Unmark(string id);

    ##
        @summary: Whether that square is inside the mark's ring. Ask it instead of doing the
        arithmetic yourself, so the ring the player sees and the ring the rule scores stay the same
        one - if they drift apart, the line on screen sits somewhere other than the line that
        counts, and nothing reports it. False for a mark with no ring, and for a square on another
        map.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function InsideMark(string id, Spot where);

    ##
        @summary: How far the server's own civil day is from UTC right now, in seconds - east of it
        positive, west of it negative. anything that turns over at midnight wants this: a daily
        reset, a weekly tax, a season all mean the operator's own midnight, and dividing World.Now
        by a day gives the wrong one everywhere but Greenwich. Add it before dividing. Read fresh,
        so a place that keeps summer time answers differently in July than in January - which is
        what keeps a boundary at midnight all year.
    ##
    public abstract integer function LocalOffset();

    ##
        @summary: One of your own values about the world itself, as a whole number, or zero for one
        never written. The place for what belongs to no body and no record - which season it is,
        whether an event is running, how many times something has happened. Read back as it was left
        when the server starts again.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Number(string key);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text. Empty for one never written.
    ##
    public abstract string function Text(string key);

    ##
        @summary: And as a yes or no. False for one never written.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Truth(string key);

    ##
        @summary: Writes one. Kept until the world is next written, which the engine does on its own
        cadence and at shutdown.
    ##
    public abstract function SetNumber(string key, integer amount);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text.
    ##
    public abstract function SetText(string key, string value);

    ##
        @summary: And as a yes or no.
    ##
    public abstract function SetTruth(string key, boolean value);

    ##
        @summary: Takes gold out of a vault, recording who spent it. Through the engine's own ledger
        rather than by writing the number: a vault that went down with nothing in the spending log
        is money a guild cannot account for. False when the vault does not hold that much, so this
        is the check as well as the payment.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function SpendGuildGold(integer guild, integer amount, Player by);

    ##
        @summary: How many records of that kind this world holds, counting blank slots. Zero for a
        kind nobody declared.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Records(string records);

    ##
        @summary: One field of one record, as text, or empty where the slot or the field is not
        there. What a game reads at run time out of the records its own editor authored.
    ##
    public abstract string function Record(string records, integer number, string field);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as a whole number. Zero where the slot or the field is not there.
    ##
    public abstract integer function RecordNumber(string records, integer number, string field);

    ##
        @summary: What a record is called - an item's name, a creature's, a map's. The one engine
        property a record can be asked for, so that a rule choosing between records can say which it
        means. Blank for a slot nobody authored. For your own records this is their name field.
    ##
    public abstract string function RecordName(string records, integer number);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as a yes or no, which is what a checkbox on the editor's form writes.
        False where the slot or the field is not there, and an unticked box gives the same answer as
        an absent one.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function RecordTruth(string records, integer number, string field);
end model

##
    @summary: The fields a message carried, handed to OnMessage. Read like a player's own keys:
    an omitted field is absent, not zero, and Has distinguishes the two. Only fields the
    message's model declared appear here.
##
abstract model Values

    ##
        @summary: Whether the message carried that field at all, so absence is told from zero.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Has(string field);

    ##
        @summary: What it carried under that name, or zero where it carried nothing.
    ##
    public abstract integer function Number(string field);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as text, or empty where it carried nothing. An enumeration field arrives
        as the member's own name.
    ##
    public abstract string function Text(string field);

    ##
        @summary: The same, as a yes or no. False where it carried nothing.
    ##
    public abstract boolean function Truth(string field);
end model

##
    @summary: A square as a single value: map, tile and plane together. Handed back by
    World.SpreadOver. Use it to pass a location around or hold a set of them, which loose
    coordinates cannot do.
##
abstract model Spot

    ##
        @summary: Which map it is on.
    ##
    public integer Map;

    ##
        @summary: How far across.
    ##
    public integer X;

    ##
        @summary: And how far down.
    ##
    public integer Y;

    ##
        @summary: Whether it is on the raised surface - a bridge, a ledge, a gantry - or on the
        ground. A bridge and the water beneath it share their coordinates, so a rule that acts on a
        square needs this to tell the two apart.
    ##
    public boolean IsRaised;
end model

##
    @summary: Something drawn on a square rather than over a body, handed back by World.Marker.
    Set its ring, label, meter and audience on the lines below; anything you leave unset is not
    drawn. Use it for a flag on a capture point, a ring around a hazard, or a name over a
    doorway.
##
abstract model Marker

    ##
        @summary: Writes that over it. Left unsaid, a mark carries no label.
    ##
    public abstract function Label(string text);

    ##
        @summary: What color it is drawn in. The pennant, the ring and the label all take it, so a
        side's own color is one line.
    ##
    public abstract function Color(integer red, integer green, integer blue);

    ##
        @summary: Draws the ground within that many tiles of it. Drawn as the staircase of tiles
        actually inside, never as a circle - so the line on the screen and World.InsideMark are one
        statement. Nothing at or below zero draws no ring.
    ##
    public abstract function Ring(integer tiles);

    ##
        @summary: A bar over the mark, that full out of that. Set the ceiling to zero for a mark
        that is only a pin, with no bar at all.
    ##
    public abstract function Meter(integer value, integer ceiling);

    ##
        @summary: Makes the mark private to those bodies. Leave it unset and everybody who can see
        the square sees it. Calling it again adds to the list, so a mark for several sides is one
        call per side. The list is a snapshot: somebody who joins after it is placed will not see
        the mark until it is placed again.
    ##
    public abstract function SeenBy(Player[] them);
end model

##
    @summary: One line of a dead creature's drop table, handed to OnLoot before it is rolled.
    Read what the world authored and write back what this kill should make of it. A line you do
    not change drops exactly as authored, free to whoever reaches it.
##
abstract model Spoils

    ##
        @summary: What this line drops, by item number.
    ##
    public integer Item;

    ##
        @summary: How many of it. Only an item that stacks reads this; anything else lands as one
        however large the number is.
    ##
    public integer Many;

    ##
        @summary: How often the line lands, as a plain percent - one in a hundred at 1, every time
        at 100 or more, never at nothing.
    ##
    public integer Chance;

    ##
        @summary: Which creature this was a copy of. What a rule about a kind of creature keys on,
        and it outlives the body - which is about to stop being there.
    ##
    public integer Kind;

    ##
        @summary: The body itself, still standing on the tile it fell on. For this call only: the
        slot is cleared the moment the table finishes rolling, and a handle kept past that answers
        IsHere with no.
    ##
    public Npc? From;

    ##
        @summary: Who killed it, or nothing when the world itself did.
    ##
    public Player? Killer;

    ##
        @summary: Sets how often the line lands, as a percentage. Zero or less skips the line
        without rolling it, so this kill owes this player nothing.
    ##
    public abstract function Rolls(integer chance);

    ##
        @summary: Makes it that many instead - a doubled purse, a halved one. Read only for an item
        that stacks, as above.
    ##
    public abstract function Yields(integer many);

    ##
        @summary: Holds the drop for that player for that long: nobody else may pick it up until the
        time runs out, and the client shows them whose it is. What stops the person who did the work
        watching somebody else walk off with it. Left unsaid, a drop is free to whoever reaches it
        first.
    ##
    public abstract function ClaimedBy(Player who, integer seconds);
end model
